      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(auth): support configurable session cookie names",
        "body": "## Why\n\nRunning two ClickClack-compatible services on one hostname currently makes their browser sessions collide because both use `cc_session` and cookies ignore ports. A foreign instance cookie can also activate ClickClack's cookie-CSRF branch, rejecting an otherwise valid login.\n\n## What changed\n\n- Added `CLICKCLACK_SESSION_COOKIE_NAME` with a validated, backward-compatible `cc_session` default.\n- Routed session writes, auth reads, and CSRF cookie detection through the same configured name.\n- Returned the effective name from desktop OAuth consume and updated desktop compatibility behavior.\n- Updated the OpenAPI/generated SDK contract, docs, and shared-hostname isolation tests.\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\nOn a live HTTPS ClickClack instance configured with `cc_stock_session`:\n\n1. Sent magic-link consume with an existing foreign `cc_session` cookie.\n2. Observed HTTP 200 instead of the prior CSRF 403.\n3. Observed only `cc_stock_session` in `Set-Cookie`, with `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, and `SameSite=Lax` preserved.\n4. Reused that cookie against `/api/me` and observed HTTP 200.\n\nThe private host and bearer values are omitted. OAuth state/challenge cookie isolation and a packaged desktop end-to-end run were not tested; those cookies are unchanged by this PR.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `go test ./...`\n- `pnpm check`\n- `pnpm build`\n- Independent attack-surface review: PASS\n- Independent contract verification: PASS, 0 findings\n\n## Risk\n\nThis changes authentication plumbing. The main invariant is that CSRF detection and auth lookup use the same single server field. Regression coverage proves a foreign default-name cookie is ignored when a custom name is configured. Empty configuration preserves `cc_session`; invalid names fail during config load.\n\n## Review state\n\nLocal repository checks and independent security/contract review are complete. GitHub CI on Node 24 and GitHub Codex review remain merge conditions.\n\n## AI assistance\n\nAI-assisted. Authored by ragesaq, code by Chisel using OpenAI GPT-5.6-Sol, with independent Anthropic and Sentinel review. The resulting code and evidence were reviewed before publication.\n",
